{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>You shall not take interest from your brother</b> — this too is an explicit commandment (Leviticus 25:36–37). Here He adds a warning also for the borrower, which is not the case in all monetary laws, where if he wishes to suffer loss to his property he is permitted to do so. But because this sin is so commonplace, He warns about it even for the borrower. And He explains here that interest from a gentile is permitted. He did not say the same regarding robbery and theft, as the Sages said (Bava Kamma 113a) that robbery from a gentile is forbidden. But interest, since it is done with the knowledge and consent of both parties, was not prohibited except on grounds of brotherhood and kindness, as He commanded (Leviticus 19:18), \"And you shall love your fellow as yourself,\" and as He said (Deuteronomy 15:9), \"Take care lest there be a matter in your heart of worthlessness,\" etc. Therefore He said, \"in order that YHWH your Elohim may bless you,\" for one who performs kindness and mercy with his brother by lending him without interest — it is accounted to him as righteousness. And likewise the remission of debts is kindness among brothers. Therefore He said (ibid. verse 3), \"you may exact from the foreigner,\" and fixed for it a blessing, for Scripture mentions the blessing only in connection with righteousness and acts of kindness — not with robbery, theft, or oppression. And He mentioned \"interest of silver, interest of food,\" to explain that one who lends a se'ah of wheat against a se'ah and a half — that is full interest even if a se'ah and a half is not worth at the time of repayment the value of the se'ah that he lent him. And He further explained, \"anything about which interest is taken\" — even with building stones and other attached items. For one might have thought that interest applies only to silver, since all things are acquired with it, and to food, which is the life of man, but regarding other things we would follow the valuation of the money at the time of the loan and of the repayment. And our Rabbis (Sifrei 129) derived from <b>you may take interest from a foreigner</b>: it is a negative prohibition that comes inclusive of a positive commandment regarding your brother, because it is permitted — since He has already said, \"You shall not take interest from your brother.\""], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:15:10.620445111Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}